  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213530",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(55,258)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $55$ modulo $258$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n55\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}258 & = & 55\\cdot 4 & + & 38 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 55 & = & 38\\cdot 1 & + & 17 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 38 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 4\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}38 & =258-55\\cdot 4 \\\\ 17 & =55-38 \\\\ 4 & =38-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =17-4\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17-(38-17\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =17\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(55-38)\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-38\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 9-(258-55\\cdot 4)\\cdot 13 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $258,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}55\\cdot 61+258\\cdot (-13) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258) \\\\ 55\\cdot 61 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,258)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $55\\cdot 61≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,258),$ which means that ${55}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,258=61.$",
